        "text": "You got it! Option B it is. \"Cheating\" with perfect, drift"
      }, {
        "text": "-free orientation is the way to go for a stable and predictable simulator.\n\nI\u0027ve integrated the **Smart Compass** IMU. Here\u0027s how it works:\n\n1.  **New Component: `IMU`**"
      }, {
        "text": "\n    *   It can be plugged into any Analog Pin (`A0` through `A5`).\n    .   It has a customizable color, just like other components.\n2.  **Physics Integration:**\n    *   The Matter"
      }, {
        "text": ".js physics engine now constantly tracks the robot\u0027s absolute angle (0-359°) in the simulation.\n    *   This angle is translated into a simulated analog voltage (0-5V) which is fed directly into the "
      }, {
        "text": "`AVRADC` peripheral.\n3.  **Code Example:**\n    *   The default Arduino code has been updated to use `analogRead(A0)` to read the IMU\u0027s angle.\n    *   It"
      }],
      "createTime": "2026-05-04T15:07:46.189Z"
    }, {
      "text": "Write IMU test code.",
      "role": "user",
      "tokenCount": 5,
      "createTime": "2026-05-04T15:08:55.812Z"
    }, {
      "text": "\n\n\nHere is a fun and practical test script to see the IMU in action! \n\nBecause the IMU gives us the exact absolute angle, we can write a **\"Shortest-Path Target Seeking\"** algorithm. This code tells the robot to turn and face a specific degree heading (like 90° East). Once it reaches the target, it pauses, picks a new target 90° away, and turns to face that one. \n\n### How to test this:\n1. Open the **Customization Lab** and click **Add** next to `IMU`. (It will default to Pin `A1`).\n2. Paste the code below into the Monaco Editor.\n3. Hit **RUN**. \n\n```cpp\n// HARDWARE PINS\nint MOTOR_LEFT \u003d 9;  \nint MOTOR_RIGHT \u003d 10; \nint IMU_PIN \u003d A1; // The virtual Analog Compass\n\nfloat targetHeading \u003d 90.0; // Start by turning to face 90 degrees\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(9600);\n  pinMode(MOTOR_LEFT, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(MOTOR_RIGHT, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(IMU_PIN, INPUT);\n  \n  Serial.println(\"IMU Test Booted!\");\n  Serial.println(\"Seeking Target: 90 degrees\");\n}\n\nfloat getHeading() {\n  // Read the raw simulated 0-5V voltage mapped to 0-1023\n  int raw \u003d analogRead(IMU_PIN);\n  \n  // Map the raw ADC value back to 0 - 360 degrees\n  return (raw / 1023.0) * 360.0;\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  float currentHeading \u003d getHeading();\n  \n  Serial.print(\"Heading: \");\n  Serial.print(currentHeading);\n  Serial.println(\" deg\");\n  \n  // Calculate the difference between where we are and where we want to be\n  float error \u003d targetHeading - currentHeading;\n  \n  // Normalize the error to -180 to 180 degrees so the robot \n  // always turns in the SHORTEST direction (doesn\u0027t do a 270 degree left turn to go 90 degrees right)\n  if (error \u003e 180) error -\u003d 360;\n  if (error \u003c -180) error +\u003d 360;\n  \n  // Simple \"Bang-Bang\" Controller\n  if (abs(error) \u003c 5.0) {\n    // We are within 5 degrees of our target! Stop the motors.\n    digitalWrite(MOTOR_LEFT, LOW);\n    digitalWrite(MOTOR_RIGHT, LOW);\n    \n    Serial.println(\"Target Reached! Pausing...\");\n    delay(2000); \n    \n    // Pick a new target 90 degrees away to keep the test going\n    targetHeading +\u003d 90.0;\n    if (targetHeading \u003e\u003d 360) targetHeading -\u003d 360; // Keep it bounded 0-360\n    \n    Serial.print(\"New Target: \");\n    Serial.println(targetHeading);\n  } \n  else if (error \u003e 0) {\n    // We need to turn Right\n    digitalWrite(MOTOR_LEFT, HIGH);\n    digitalWrite(MOTOR_RIGHT, LOW);\n  } \n  else {\n    // We need to turn Left\n    digitalWrite(MOTOR_LEFT, LOW);\n    digitalWrite(MOTOR_RIGHT, HIGH);\n  }\n  \n  delay(20); // Small delay for stability\n}\n```\n\n### What to watch for:\n* Watch the **Serial Monitor**: You will see the heading updating rapidly. The moment it gets within 5 degrees of the target, the motors snap off.\n* Watch the **SYS_TELEMETRY Angle**: You can verify that the raw physics engine angle matches the math output in your serial monitor!\n* Try **manually dragging** the robot around with your mouse while the code is running. The robot will instantly fight you and attempt to realign itself to the target heading!",
      "role": "model",
      "finishReason": "STOP",
      "tokenCount": 927,
      "parts": [{
        "text": "\n\n\nHere is a fun and practical test script to see the IMU in action!"
      }, {
