        "text": "This is a perfect"
      }, {
        "text": " plan. Breaking it down into steps ensures we get the UI and layout perfectly smooth before we dive into the hardcore I2C hardware"
      }, {
        "text": " emulation.\n\nHere is what I have done in this update:\n1.  **Added Resizable Divider:** There"
      }, {
        "text": " is now a vertical drag handle between the Customization Lab and the Simulation Controller. You can resize it just like the top/bottom and"
      }, {
        "text": " left/right dividers!\n2.  **Added Displays to Customization Lab:** You can now select **\"LCD 16x2 ("
      }, {
        "text": "I2C)\"** or **\"OLED 128x64 (I2C)\"** from"
      }, {
        "text": " the component dropdown. \n    *   They act like physical chips on the robot (small customizable squares) that represent"
      }, {
        "text": " the hardware connection to the `SDA/SCL (A4/A5)` pins.\n3.  **"
      }, {
        "text": "Summoning the Screen:** If you add either display to your robot, a beautiful Virtual Screen UI summons directly above the RUN"
      }, {
        "text": "/STOP buttons. If you delete the component from the robot, the screen smoothly disappears. \n    *   *"
      }, {
        "text": "The LCD features a classic retro green-backlight aesthetic.*\n    *   *The OLED features a sleek dark blue"
      }, {
        "text": "/cyan aesthetic.*\n\n*(Note: They are just visual placeholders right now while we confirm the layout. Once you approve,"
      }, {
        "text": " we will wire up the virtual I2C peripheral to intercept the HD44780 and SSD1306 data"
      }, {
